node.propertyBindings.put(Properties.PLAYBACK_STATE, c.playing());
...
node.mediaInput = new MediaInput() {
    @Override
    public void onPlayStateChanged(boolean playing) {
        c.playing().set(playing);
    }
    ...
};
```

Yes! The play-state report is fed into the **same** `playing` command signal. That violates the spec's commands-vs-reports rule (and is exactly why the app's `playing` becomes false on end).

The spec says PLAYBACK_STATE is a command; the app writes it. The report `MEDIA_PLAY_STATE_CHANGED` should flow into a **report** signal (like `time`, `volumeReport`, `duration`), not the command. The Java `MediaController` has `time()` (report for position), `duration()`, `volumeReport()`, but **no** `playStateReport()`/`playingReport()`. So the design conflates. Hmm. Let me check the Rust `MediaController` (pathland-engine) — it has `playing` (command) and maybe a separate report? Earlier `media.rs` fields: playing, position, volume, muted, loop, rate, time, duration, volume_report. No `playing_report`. And `handle_event`? Let me check how the Rust engine routes MEDIA_PLAY_STATE_CHANGED. If the Rust engine also writes into `playing`, then the same bug exists in the Rust DSL demo (and maybe the spec's reference). But the spec's §4.2 explicitly warns against it. Hmm.

